IMPORT prelude

-- Income Tax Assessment Act 1997 (Commonwealth of Australia) -- depreciation amounts a sole trader or landlord meets, computed
-- from the formulas the Federal Register prints as images: the decline in value of a depreciating asset by the diminishing
-- value method (ss 40-70(1), 40-72(1), (3)) and the prime cost method (s 40-75(1), (7)), and the deduction for a general small
-- business pool (s 328-190(1)).
--
-- A module beside itaa-business.l4 (24 October 2026). Depreciation was outside the baseline ("Everything outside these lives:
-- depreciation"); a business owner meets it in every return, so it is added here. Nothing in itaa-business.l4 changes.
--
-- SEAT: the taxpayer -- an individual who holds a depreciating asset for a business or rental ("How much can I deduct for the
-- decline in value of my van?").
--
-- Edition: Compilation No. 267 (register id C2026C00400), as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/C2004A05138.txt. The
-- formulas are transcribed in ../../registers/source-bundle/C2004A05138.formulas.md (entries s 40-70(1), s 40-72(1),
-- s 40-75(1) and its Example, s 328-190(1); recovered 2026-10-24), and encoded from there.
--
-- NOT ENCODED. What is a depreciating asset, its cost and its effective life (Subdivs 40-B, 40-C; effective life asked as a
-- fact, in years and fractions of years); the taxable-use reduction (s 40-25(2)); the adjustments to the prime cost formula
-- (s 40-75(2) to (6)); balancing adjustments; low-value pools (s 40-425); s 328-190(2) to (4) and the low pool value rule
-- (s 328-210); s 328-225 (change in business use). Who may use Subdivision 328-D (a small business entity) is one fact.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written with the writing-l4-rules skill from the individual's seat with the 2026-10-18 defaults. No
-- pipeline, no coverage table, no independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Income Tax Assessment Act 1997 -- depreciation from the formulas`

GIVEN `a` IS A NUMBER, `b` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the lower figure of` MEANS
    IF `a` AT MOST `b` THEN `a` ELSE `b`

§§ `40-70, 40-72 -- diminishing value method`

-- s 40-72(1), for an asset you started to hold on or after 10 May 2006, from the authorised rendering:
--     *Base value × (Days held / 365) × (200% / Asset's *effective life)
-- s 40-70(1), for an asset held earlier: the same with 150%. "base value is: (a) for the income year in which the asset's *start
-- time occurs -- its *cost; or (b) for a later year -- the sum of its *opening adjustable value for that year and any amount
-- included in the second element of its cost for that year." "days held is the number of days you *held the asset in the income
-- year from its *start time, ignoring any days ... when you did not use the asset, or have it *installed ready for use".
-- s 40-72(3): "The decline in value ... cannot be more than the amount that is the asset's *base value for that income year."
@export What is the decline in value of my asset this income year by the diminishing value method (ss 40-70, 40-72)?
GIVEN `started to hold on or after 10 May 2006` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you started to hold the asset on or after 10 May 2006
      `base value` IS A NUMBER @desc The asset's cost in its first year; later, its opening adjustable value plus any second-element cost for the year, in dollars
      `days held` IS A NUMBER @desc The days in the income year you held the asset from its start time, leaving out days it was not used or installed ready for use
      `effective life in years` IS A NUMBER @desc The asset's effective life, in years and fractions of years
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the decline in value by the diminishing value method` MEANS
    `the lower figure of` `base value` ((`base value` TIMES `days held` TIMES `the percentage`) DIVIDED BY (365 TIMES 100 TIMES `effective life in years`))
    WHERE
        `the percentage` MEANS IF `started to hold on or after 10 May 2006` THEN 200 ELSE 150

§§ `40-75 -- prime cost method`

-- s 40-75(1), from the authorised rendering:   Asset's *cost × (Days held / 365) × (100% / Asset's *effective life)
-- (7): "The decline in value ... cannot be more than: (a) for the income year in which the asset's *start time occurs -- its
-- *cost; or (b) for a later year -- the sum of its *opening adjustable value for that year and any amount included in the second
-- element of its cost for that year." The adjustments of (2) to (6) are not encoded.
@export What is the decline in value of my asset this income year by the prime cost method (s 40-75(1), (7))?
GIVEN `cost` IS A NUMBER @desc The asset's cost, in dollars
      `days held` IS A NUMBER @desc The days in the income year you held the asset from its start time, leaving out days it was not used or installed ready for use
      `effective life in years` IS A NUMBER @desc The asset's effective life, in years and fractions of years
      `the most it can decline this year` IS A NUMBER @desc In the first year, the cost; later, the opening adjustable value plus any second-element cost for the year, in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the decline in value by the prime cost method` MEANS
    `the lower figure of` `the most it can decline this year` ((`cost` TIMES `days held`) DIVIDED BY (365 TIMES `effective life in years`))

§§ `328-190(1) -- general small business pool`

-- s 328-190(1), from the authorised rendering: "You calculate your deduction for your *general small business pool for an income
-- year using this formula:   *Opening pool balance × 30%". The Note: "You use section 328-210 instead if the pool has a low pool
-- value" (not encoded).
@export What can I deduct for my general small business pool this income year (s 328-190(1))?
GIVEN `opening pool balance` IS A NUMBER @desc The pool's opening balance for the income year (s 328-195), in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What can I deduct for my small business pool` MEANS
    (`opening pool balance` TIMES 30) DIVIDED BY 100
